 \textbf{Simulation methods and tools (T1.1--T1.3, T2.1, T2.2, T3.3).} Our custom Python codes combine semi-analytical simulation of guided membrane modes and their gas overlap $\Gamma$ (e.g.~0.45 at 500 nm SiN$_x$, Fig.~1d), oblique-incidence transfer-matrix models of the in-plane DBR mirrors, and 2D ray tracing and Poincar\'e mapping of the multipass cells (Fig.~2). It builds on our integrated circular-cell model with index perturbations, whose coherently summed speckle field is very sensitive to phase \cite{Arola2026}. The noise model (Figs.~2f and 3b) gives the noise floor and detection limit from the averaged residual fringe and the shot noise, and the decorrelation width is compared with the Fourier transform of $p(L)$, testing RO1. All designs use TM polarization only, since TE has a Brewster window of incidence angles with no DBR reflection.
 
-\textbf{Preliminary results.} \jc{Computer simulations of chaotic membrane cells (10\,mm across, 3.3\,\textmu{}m light, realistic mirror and membrane losses) show that each dither removes a further part of the interference noise, the ``fringe'' (RO1, Fig.~2f). The remaining fringe falls from $3.6\times10^{-4}$ without dither to $1.8\times10^{-5}$ with wavelength dither, $1.6\times10^{-6}$ when the beam angle and position are added, and $1.5\times10^{-7}$ when the membrane index is also modulated, below our target of $5\times10^{-7}$. The input dither works only if the beam is wide enough to be launched in many distinct ways: a small tilt of a narrow beam gains nothing. A Herriott-type cell of the same length reaches $1.4\times10^{-6}$ but offers only a third of the absorption path. For RO3, the simulated methane noise floor reaches 1.4 ppb in 100 ms with 10 mW and all dithers (Fig.~3b), and 1.7 ppb for ethane; at 1 mW the detector limits it to 13/17 ppb. The 3.3\,\textmu{}m lines are about 250 times stronger than those at 1.64\,\textmu{}m, which motivates the wavelength staging (Fig.~3a). The results scale with the stray light in the cell, measured in T2.3, and the heating side effects of the index modulation are not yet modelled. The spread between cell layouts is large (Fig.~3b), so layout choice is part of the design (T1.1, T1.2). The code and data are described in a preprint \cite{CMPCpreprint}.}
